[2026-03-15T01:29:59.313Z] {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XgW7wXc8Y64osXFmMPKXTL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The \"Submit button is disabled when ratings are not set\" test is failing because the page shows \"You have already reviewed this booking\" instead of the review form. This is a data contamination issue - a previous test submitted a review for Alex's booking, and the `beforeEach` cleanup isn't working.\n\nLooking at the test:\n```ts\ntest.beforeEach(async ({ page }) => {\n    await deleteAllReviewsForGuest(page, ALEX_ID)\n})\n```\n\nThe `deleteAllReviewsForGuest` uses `page.request.get('/api/reviews?guest_id=...')` - but `page.request` with relative URLs needs the page to have been navigated somewhere first (not `about:blank`). \n\nI initially thought this was the issue mentioned in testing mandate #12 about navigating before `page.evaluate(fetch(...))`, but `page.request` is actually Playwright's APIRequestContext which should work with a baseURL configured in the playwright config. Let me verify the actual implementation of the cleanup function to see what's really happening.\n\nThe `deleteAllReviewsForGuest` function is using `page.request` to fetch and delete reviews via API endpoints, which should respect the baseURL setting. But I'm noticing that previous tests pass fine with this pattern, so the real problem might be a race condition where another test submitted a review for Alex's booking, the beforeEach cleanup ran, but then something else happened with the \"Submit button is disabled when ratings are\" test.\n\nActually, I think I see it now — tests are running in parallel, and since they're all modifying the same booking's reviews in the database, they're interfering with each other. The test that submits a review is running concurrently with the disabled button test, so a review gets created between when beforeEach cleans up and when the page loads. The describe block isn't using `test.describe.serial`, and the playwright config has `fullyParallel: true`, which means tests run with multiple workers. But that conflicts with the test isolation requirement that says to use `test.describe.serial` by default for tests that share state. Hmm, looking more carefully at the test structure: The `beforeEach` deletes all reviews for Alex. But in a parallel run, test A's beforeEach deletes reviews, then test B's beforeEach also deletes reviews, then test A submits a review, and by the time test \"Submit button is disabled when ratings are not set\" navigates to the review page, the review from another test may already be submitted.\n\nBut wait - the tests are in the same file with fullyParallel. Actually, in Playwright, `fullyParallel: true` means tests within a single file can run in parallel too. These tests all modify the same data (Alex's reviews), so they interfere with each other.\n\nThe real question is: was this test previously passing? The task says \"FixTests: tests/write-review-form.spec.ts\" so maybe it was flaky. This looks like a flaky parallel execution issue.\n\nLooking at the first run, 12 passed and only 1 failed. Now after my fix, a different test fails - that's a sign of data contamination between parallel tests. The issue is that Test A submits a review after Test B's beforeEach runs but before Test B navigates to the review page, causing the \"already reviewed\" state to appear unexpectedly. I need to make these tests serial by wrapping them in `test.describe.serial` since they all operate on the same booking and user reviews.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":548,"cache_read_input_tokens":51786,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":548,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f631ecaf-1726-48a2-8606